<b>ז.</b> "The Akedah." Rabbeinu Bachya writes (Tzav) that mere recitation is insufficient; one must understand the section and recognize God's wonders. This is also the meaning of "whoever says Ashrei three times daily is assured a share in the World to Come."<br>
<br>
<b>ח.</b> "Olah." After olah, shelamim, and todah, one should say the libations section (Parashat Shelach), for no sacrifice is without libations; but chatat and asham do not require libations. Parashat Tzav itself does not belong to voluntary olah but to the daily offering (see se'if 9). One should also say "ve-sham'ah kol alah" at the end of Vayikra, concerning oaths and guilt offerings. Why do Tur and Beit Yosef list olah before chatat, though chatat usually precedes? In Zevachim 7b, chatat first is like an advocate entering before the gift. In our recitation, however, Tur says not to conclude chatat with "Yehi ratzon" (perhaps one is not liable), while olah atones for neglected positive commands and applies broadly. Therefore olah may be said first in this framework; but one who knows he is liable for chatat should say chatat first.<br>
<br>
<b>ט.</b> "In public." Because of heretics who say only this is Torah, especially in our times; therefore we do not print it in a special public booklet (Lechem Chamudot).
